cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Paralome, please cite it as below."
title: "Paralome: a reproducible, checkpointed HPC workflow framework for omics analysis"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Jones
    given-names: Kenneth L.
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-1289-4652"
    email: Ken.Jones@bioinformaticsolutions.com
license: AGPL-3.0-only
repository-code: "https://github.com/BioinformaticSolutions/paralome"
url: "https://www.bioinformaticsolutions.com/paralome.html"
abstract: >-
  Paralome is a reproducible, checkpointed framework for omics analysis that
  runs on an HPC cluster (SLURM) or a single machine. One command sets up an
  analysis and submits it stage by stage, and every stage is checkpointed, so
  an interrupted or extended analysis resumes rather than restarts.
  Independent pipelines -- bulk RNA-seq and ATAC-seq; exome and whole-genome
  variant calling with family segregation; single-cell RNA and single-nucleus
  ATAC, including spatial, from curated Seurat objects; and proteomics,
  phosphoproteomics and Olink -- share one checkpoint backbone and one
  statistics module. Differential statistics (Student's t, paired t,
  multi-factor ANOVA and linear models, on pseudobulk or individual cells) are
  stated by the user on every run. Single-cell analyses add lineage
  trajectories, and results carry functional evidence from Evidome and are
  delivered as self-describing archives, written for an AI agent to interpret
  and for people to open in OmicView.
keywords:
  - bioinformatics
  - HPC
  - SLURM
  - workflow
  - pipeline
  - RNA-seq
  - ATAC-seq
  - single-cell
  - spatial transcriptomics
  - variant calling
  - proteomics
  - phosphoproteomics
  - Olink
  - trajectories
  - functional evidence

